{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c001", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The preamble carries a stray quotation mark and the dangling phrase \"(“Agreement”) as further defined below,\" which points to a definition of \"Agreement\" that never appears in Section 1. A court could be asked what instrument, and what effective date (January 1, 2009 versus the undated signatures), the parties actually bound themselves to.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.14}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c002", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c003", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"PRODUCTS\" is defined only as those products \"authorized by COMPANY,\" with no schedule, list or writing fixing what is authorized at any moment. Because COMPANY may also \"eliminate any PRODUCT for any reason,\" the subject matter of the sale obligation is indeterminate.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c004", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The definition confines DISTRIBUTOR to \"retail grocery store outlet customers only\" as the AUTHORIZED CHANNEL OF TRADE, which cannot be squared with Section 3(ii)(a)(1)(B) and Exhibit B, both of which authorize sales to Kmart, a mass merchandiser. A court would have to decide whether Kmart sales are inside or outside the defined channel for purposes of the Section 12(iii)(a) termination right.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.38}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c005", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Good Industry Practice\" is defined by reference to standards \"universally accepted and followed in the baking industry,\" a benchmark that is both unverifiable and drawn from a different industry than snack distribution. Because Section 12(iii)(b) makes failure to operate in accordance with Good Industry Practice a ground for termination, the vagueness is consequential.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c006", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The term clause says the Agreement \"shall continue until December 31, 2010, and shall continue in effect until either party terminates,\" so it is unclear whether the Agreement expires on that date or renews indefinitely subject only to ninety days' notice. The phrase \"at least ninety (90) days prior to such termination\" also leaves open who fixes the termination date.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.55},
    {"issue": "Section 2 permits either party to end the Agreement on ninety days' written notice without stating any cause, while Section 12(i) permits termination on ninety days' notice only \"for good cause\" or on a change in COMPANY's business. A court would have to decide whether termination without cause is permitted, notwithstanding the \"Subject to Section 12\" opening words.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.42}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c007", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Section 3(i) lets COMPANY \"determine the TERRITORY and AUTHORIZED CHANNEL OF TRADE\" unilaterally during the term, while Section 1(iii) fixes the TERRITORY by reference to Exhibit A and Section 15(iii) permits modification only by a writing executed by both parties. Which provision controls a unilateral shrinking of the territory would have to be construed.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c008", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The closed list \"shall (and may only) Sell to: grocery store retail outlets only; Kmart; specific customers as approved by COMPANY\" cannot be reconciled with the Section 1(iii) definition limiting the AUTHORIZED CHANNEL OF TRADE to retail grocery outlets. Whether Kmart and COMPANY-approved accounts are within or outside the authorized channel drives the cure and termination provisions of Section 12(iii)(a).", "type": 2, "prob": 0.32}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c009", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The flat prohibition on \"Wholesaler operations whereby 'in-store service' is not provided\" sits against Section 15(ii)(a), which says nothing in the Agreement prohibits DISTRIBUTOR from conducting business through subdistributors. A court would have to decide whether use of independent subdistributors is permitted business or prohibited wholesaling.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c010", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The acknowledgment that DISTRIBUTOR has no \"exclusive rights to any stores or designated geography\" is hard to square with the Section 1(iii) and Exhibit A grant of an \"authorized\" TERRITORY and CUSTOMERS. A court would have to determine what the territorial grant confers if it confers no exclusivity at all.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c011", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The prohibition reads that DISTRIBUTOR \"shall not sell the PRODUCTS ... to any CUSTOMERS or in any AUTHORIZED CHANNEL OF TRADE other than as stated herein,\" which literally forbids sales in the very channel the Agreement authorizes. The sentence \"any such changes are effective thirty days of receipt\" is likewise missing a word fixing when Exhibit B revisions take effect.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.33}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c012", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The clause promises \"commercially reasonable efforts to resolve\" conflicting sales that violate \"Paragraph 3(a)(i) above,\" but there is no Paragraph 3(a)(i) in the Agreement. The mis-citation leaves it unclear which sales trigger COMPANY's obligation, and \"commercially reasonable efforts\" is given no benchmark.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.35}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c013", "issues": [
    {"issue": "COMPANY's unqualified promise to \"deliver the PRODUCTS to DISTRIBUTOR\" cannot be squared with Section 3(ii)(g), which lets COMPANY cancel any delivery in its sole discretion, impose \"cuts,\" and discontinue items without any liability. Whether there is an enforceable supply obligation at all is a question for construction.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.40}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c014", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Any cause beyond its control\" is an unbounded catch-all with no enumerated events to give it content, and it is coupled to the equally open standard \"cannot be overcome by reasonable diligence.\" A court would have to decide whether ordinary business events such as plant or supply decisions fall inside it.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c015", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The unrestricted right to cancel deliveries, impose \"cuts\" and discontinue items \"without incurring any liability\" cannot be reconciled with COMPANY's delivery obligation in Section 3(ii)(e) or the shipping commitment in Section 6. A court would have to decide whether this clause reduces those obligations to illusory promises.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.35}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c016", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c017", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c018", "issues": [
    {"issue": "COMPANY's reserved right to sell within the TERRITORY \"to customers other than the CUSTOMERS\" and to deliver to a CUSTOMER's central or district warehouse sits against the territorial and customer grant in Section 1(iii) and Exhibit A. Whether COMPANY may supply the distributor's own accounts through their warehouses would require construction of the two together.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c019", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The clause grants inspection \"without notice\" and \"at any time during normal business hours\" while simultaneously promising \"reasonable efforts not to disturb the operations of DISTRIBUTOR.\" The two standards pull against one another and \"reasonable efforts\" is left undefined.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.15}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c020", "issues": [
