  {"clause_id": "c051", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"General market information regarding the TERRITORY\" is undefined and unbounded, giving no way to tell what DISTRIBUTOR must produce on request. The obligation also sits oddly with the confidentiality of DISTRIBUTOR's own customer data.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.15}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c052", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The confidentiality obligation covers \"any and all business and technical information pertaining to the COMPANY and/or the PRODUCTS\" with no carve-out for publicly known or independently obtained information and no time limit. What information is captured, and for how long, is left to argument.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c053", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Section 12(i) permits termination on ninety days' notice only \"for good cause\" or on a change in COMPANY's business, which cannot be squared with Section 2's provision that the Agreement continues \"until either party terminates ... by providing ... written notice of termination at least ninety (90) days prior.\" Whether termination without cause was available is the central construction question.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.45}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c054", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This subsection allows COMPANY to terminate \"immediately\" for the listed events, while Section 12(i) states that the Agreement may be terminated \"by either party at any time upon ninety (90) days written notice ... for good cause.\" Whether a listed event permits immediate termination or still requires ninety days' notice is unresolved on the face of Section 12.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c055", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The five-business-day notice and cure mechanism for non-payment and out-of-channel selling cannot be squared with Section 12(i), which conditions termination for \"good cause\" on ninety days' written notice. Which notice period a terminating COMPANY must observe is a question the two provisions leave open.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c056", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The catch-all covers any failure \"which, in the sole discretion of COMPANY, are material,\" leaving materiality wholly to the terminating party, and pairs a five-day cure period with an undefined \"longer, commercially reasonable period\" for breaches not curable in five days. Which category a given default falls into, and how long the cure period then runs, is indeterminate.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.32}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c057", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The repurchase option is at COMPANY's option and at \"DISTRIBUTOR'S net warehouse cost,\" a figure the Agreement nowhere defines, and the sentence closes with a stray \"for.).\" What DISTRIBUTOR is entitled to receive for remaining inventory on termination is therefore uncertain.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c058", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The sentence bars liability for \"consequential, punitive or incidental damages ... in any cumulative amount greater than the sum of 50% of DISTRIBUTOR'S aggregate gross sales\" in the preceding twelve months, so it is unclear whether the 50% figure is a cap on all damages or only on the excluded categories. It is also unclear whether an exclusion and a cap can coexist, since damages of the listed kinds appear both barred and permitted up to the cap.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.52}
